<div class="section-header" id="salsa">

    <span class="section-kicker">
        SALSA
    </span>

    <h2>
        Why the World Calls Cali the Salsa Capital
    </h2>

</div>

<p>
    Contrary to what many visitors believe, salsa was not born in Cali. By the time the genre reached Colombia, it had already evolved through Cuban rhythms, Puerto Rican musicians, and the vibrant Latin communities of New York City.
</p>

<p>
    What happened in Cali was something entirely different. The city didn't invent salsa: it made it its own. More importantly, it developed a completely unique way of dancing it, known today as <strong>salsa caleña</strong>.
</p>

<p>
    Since salsa first arrived here, generations of caleños have continuously reinvented the dance, creating increasingly fast, creative and footwork-focused styles that you simply won't find anywhere else in the world. Watching a couple dance in Cali is very different from watching salsa in Cuba, Puerto Rico or New York.
</p>

<p>
    Many local historians also point out that salsa found fertile ground in Cali during a period of profound social change. As thousands of people migrated to the city (especially Afro-Colombian communities from Colombia's Pacific region) the city's cultural identity evolved rapidly. Combined with Buenaventura, Colombia's main Pacific port and the gateway through which countless vinyl records entered the country, Cali developed an extraordinary connection with Afro-Caribbean music.
</p>

<div class="editorial-quote">

```
<p>
    Cali didn't become the world's salsa capital because it produced the most famous salsa bands.
</p>

<h3>
    It became the world's salsa capital because it reinvented the way the world dances salsa.
</h3>

<p class="editorial-quote__footer">
    Generations of extraordinary dancers, passionate record collectors and music lovers transformed salsa into something that became part of everyday life.
</p>
```

</div>


<p> Today, salsa is far more than something you'll see on stage. It's woven into everyday life. You'll hear it in taxis, neighborhood stores, family gatherings, cafés and parks. And if you happen to visit during late December, you'll experience the <strong>Feria de Cali</strong>, the city's biggest celebration and one of the most important salsa festivals in the world. For an entire week, Cali becomes a giant dance floor where locals and visitors celebrate the music that has shaped the city's identity for generations. </p>

<p> Beyond the dancers, Cali is also home to one of the world's most passionate communities of <strong>melómanos</strong>: music lovers and vinyl collectors who have spent decades preserving, studying and sharing salsa recordings from across Latin America and the Caribbean. Their passion has helped keep the genre alive, influencing generations of dancers and making recorded music an essential part of Cali's salsa culture. </p>

<p> Whether you already dance or have never taken a single lesson, experiencing salsa in Cali is one of the best ways to understand the city itself. It isn't simply entertainment: it is one of the languages through which Cali expresses its history, diversity and personality. </p>

