<div class="section-header" id="nature">

    <span class="section-kicker">
        NATURE
    </span>

    <h2>
        Nature in Cali
    </h2>

</div>

<p>
    One of the biggest surprises for first-time visitors is how naturally Cali blends into its surroundings. While many cities treat nature as something you have to drive hours to reach, in Cali it simply becomes part of everyday life. The city sits between the Andes Mountains and Colombia's Pacific region, one of the most biodiverse places on Earth, making wildlife and green landscapes an ordinary part of life for locals.
</p>

<p>
    Every afternoon, usually around 4 p.m., flocks of parrots and macaws fly across the city, filling the air with their unmistakable calls. Wherever there are tall palm trees, chances are you'll hear them. Colombia is home to more species of palm trees than any other country in the world, and Cali benefits from that extraordinary natural richness.
</p>

<div class="editorial-quote">

    <p>
        In Cali, nature isn't somewhere you go.
    </p>

    <h3>
        It's simply part of everyday life.
    </h3>

</div>

<p>
    The Cali River flows directly through the city and is considered one of Colombia's cleanest urban rivers. It is even home to a small population of wild river otters, something few visitors expect to find in the middle of a city of more than two million people.
</p>

<p>
    Birdwatchers from around the world travel to Colombia, and Cali has quietly become one of the country's best gateways into that world. Less than an hour from downtown, the cloud forests of the Farallones de Cali National Park are home to extraordinary species, including the Multicolored Tanager, an endemic bird found only in this part of Colombia. The region is also famous for its hummingbirds. In fact, few places on Earth offer such an impressive diversity of hummingbird species within such a short distance from a major city.
</p>

<p>
    For locals, these mountains are more than a birdwatching destination. They're where people escape the tropical heat. After just a 45 minute drive, temperatures drop dramatically, the air becomes cooler, and traditional countryside lodges and glampings offer one of the most peaceful experiences near the city.
</p>

<p>
    Back in the city, the Cali Zoo is widely regarded as one of the finest zoological parks in Latin America. Beautifully integrated into the surrounding landscape, it combines excellent conservation work with one of Colombia's most diverse collections of native wildlife.
</p>

<p>
    No guide to nature around Cali would be complete without mentioning Pance. Every weekend, thousands of locals head to its crystal clear mountain river to cool off, relax with family and friends, or, as many jokingly admit, recover from a Saturday night filled with salsa and aguardiente. The water is so clean that many people still drink directly from the streams flowing down from the mountains.
</p>

<p>
    And if you have an extra day, San Cipriano is one of the most unforgettable destinations in the region. Surrounded by dense tropical rainforest, this small village showcases why Colombia is considered one of the world's biodiversity hotspots. Exotic birds such as the spectacular Oropendola are commonly seen around the village, while rivers, jungle trails and the famous motorbike powered 'brujitas' rail carts create an experience unlike anywhere.
</p>

<p>
    Whether you're interested in birdwatching, waterfalls, hiking, cloud forests, mountain rivers or simply escaping the city for a few hours, Cali offers a side of Colombia that many travelers never expect to find.
</p>

