<div class="section-header" id="food">

    <span class="section-kicker">
        FOOD
    </span>

    <h2>
        Food in Cali
    </h2>

</div>

<p>
    Many Colombians will tell you that Valle del Cauca has one of the country's finest cuisines. Whether that's objectively true is impossible to say, but what is undeniable is that Cali's food tells the story of the people who built this city.
</p>

<p>
    Humans are naturally social. Wherever cultures meet, ideas are exchanged, traditions evolve, and new flavors emerge. Cali is one of Colombia's most diverse cities, shaped by centuries of migration and known today as the capital of southwestern Colombia. Its gastronomy reflects that extraordinary cultural blend.
</p>

<div class="editorial-quote">

    <p>
        Cali's cuisine isn't defined by a single tradition.
    </p>

    <h3>
        It's the result of centuries of cultures sharing the same table.
    </h3>

</div>

<p>
    Spanish and European influence can still be found in the region's traditional desserts, many of them made from sugar cane grown throughout the valley. Delicacies such as <strong>manjar blanco</strong>, <strong>dulce cortado</strong> and <strong>desamargado</strong> remain an essential part of local celebrations and family gatherings.
</p>

<p>
    From Colombia's Pacific coast comes a strong Afro-Colombian influence, particularly visible in seafood. Fresh ceviches, rich fish stews and dishes like <strong>arroz atollado</strong> can be enjoyed everywhere from traditional markets such as Alameda to some of the city's finest restaurants.
</p>

<p>
    Indigenous heritage is equally present through the incredible diversity of tropical fruits found in the region. Chirimoya, naidí, local blueberries, cacao and dozens of other native fruits appear in juices, desserts and refreshing drinks. Two local favorites are the famous <strong>cholado</strong>, a colorful dessert made with shaved ice, fruit and syrups, and the equally iconic <strong>lulada</strong>, a refreshing drink prepared with fresh lulo, ice and sugar.
</p>

<p>
    No visit to Cali would be complete without experiencing its beloved <strong>fritanga</strong>. Empanadas, marranitas, papa rellena and aborrajados are part of everyday life, whether enjoyed as a quick breakfast, an afternoon snack or after a night of salsa dancing. Add the enormous bread culture, excellent rotisserie chicken restaurants and the legendary <strong>chuleta valluna</strong>, and you'll quickly understand why locals take such pride in their food.
</p>

<p>
    Perhaps one of Cali's greatest culinary strengths is its accessibility. Whether you're buying an empanada from a neighborhood bakery or enjoying a tasting menu in San Antonio, Ciudad Jardín, El Peñón, Parque del Perro or Granada, you'll find remarkable quality at prices that are often a fraction of what you'd expect in many other Colombian cities.
</p>

