<div class="section-header" id="afro-culture">

    <span class="section-kicker">
        AFRO CULTURE
    </span>

    <h2>
        Afro-Colombian Culture
    </h2>

</div>

<p>
    It is impossible to understand Cali without understanding its Afro-Colombian heritage. The city is home to one of the largest Afro-descendant populations in the Americas and the largest in Colombia, making Black culture not a niche or minority expression, but one of the foundations of everyday life.
</p>

<p>
    Roughly one out of every three people in Cali identifies as Afro-Colombian. For many visitors from the African diaspora, especially from North America and the Caribbean, this creates an immediate sense of familiarity. It is common to hear visitors say that Cali is one of the few places where they feel they can blend in rather than stand out.
</p>

<div class="editorial-quote">

    <p>
        To understand Cali...
    </p>

    <h3>
        ...you first have to understand its Afro-Colombian soul.
    </h3>

</div>

<p>
    Diversity is simply part of everyday life here. Music, language, dance, cuisine and even humor have all been profoundly shaped by Afro-Colombian communities, particularly those whose families migrated from Colombia's Pacific Coast throughout the twentieth century.
</p>

<p>
    Locals often repeat a famous phrase borrowed from a classic salsa song: <em>"Sin negro no hay guaguancó."</em> Literally, it means "Without Black people there is no guaguancó," but culturally it expresses something much deeper: without Afro-descendant communities, there would be no party, no rhythm and no Cali as we know it today.
</p>

<p>
    Unlike many European cities, where Black communities are largely the result of recent migration, Cali's Afro-descendant population traces its roots back centuries to the colonial period and the transatlantic slave trade. Much of their original African heritage was intentionally suppressed during colonization, yet countless traditions survived and evolved. They remain visible today in music, dance, food, language, spirituality and countless everyday customs.
</p>

<p>
    Their influence extends throughout the city, but becomes especially visible in eastern Cali, where many neighborhoods have predominantly Afro-Colombian populations and where cultural traditions continue to thrive through music, family life and community celebrations.
</p>

<p>
    Whether you're dancing salsa, listening to Pacific music, enjoying traditional seafood or simply walking through the city, you're experiencing a culture that has been profoundly shaped by generations of Afro-Colombians whose contributions continue to define Cali's identity.
</p>

