<div class="section-header" id="where-to-stay">

    <span class="section-kicker">
        WHERE TO STAY
    </span>

    <h2>
        Where to Stay in Cali
    </h2>

</div>

<p>
    Choosing where to stay in Cali is probably one of the most important decisions you'll make before your trip. The city has more than two million inhabitants, and the overwhelming majority of its neighborhoods are residential, where everyday local life unfolds far away from the tourist areas.
</p>

<p>
    That creates two very different experiences. You can stay in Cali's traditional visitor neighborhoods, where restaurants, museums, nightlife and historic landmarks are often within walking distance. Or you can choose a more residential area, offering a much more authentic local experience, usually at a lower price, but requiring Uber rides to reach most attractions.
</p>

<div class="editorial-quote">

    <p>
        The best neighborhood doesn't exist.
    </p>

    <h3>
        The best neighborhood depends entirely on the kind of trip you want to have.
    </h3>

</div>

<h3>Historic Cali</h3>

<p>
    For most first-time visitors, this is where we'd recommend staying. San Antonio, El Peñón and Granada form the traditional tourist heart of the city, combining history, restaurants, nightlife, boutique hotels and easy access to many of Cali's main attractions.
</p>

<p>
    <strong>San Antonio</strong> is perhaps Cali's most iconic neighborhood. Colonial houses, quiet streets, cafés and panoramic viewpoints create one of the city's most charming atmospheres. Around half of Cali's backpacker hostels are located here, alongside beautiful Airbnbs and boutique hotels such as Casa Níspero and Hotel Terraza San Antonio.
</p>

<p>
    <strong>El Peñón</strong>, immediately next to San Antonio, feels slightly more modern. Here you'll find international restaurants, cocktail bars, upscale accommodation and some of Cali's best hotels, including the InterContinental Cali, Hotel Dann Carlton, Hotel El Peñón by Bit Hotels, Hype States, Villa del Peñón Suites and Hotel Obelisco.
</p>

<p>
    <strong>Granada</strong> is another favorite, particularly among European visitors. Flat, walkable and filled with restaurants, cafés, boutique hotels and nightlife, it also hosts international chains such as the Marriott and Ibis, together with excellent independent hotels like Hotel Now and Hotel Leclerc.
</p>

<p><strong>Best for:</strong> First-time visitors • Walkability • Restaurants • Salsa • Nightlife</p>

<hr>

<h3>Medical & Luxury Cali</h3>

<p>
    Ciudad Jardín has become the preferred area for many international visitors travelling to Cali for dental treatments or medical tourism. It is home to numerous private clinics, luxury restaurants, shopping malls and elegant residential developments.
</p>

<p>
    Unlike Historic Cali, however, Ciudad Jardín is built around the car. Distances are much greater, making Uber almost essential for sightseeing. On the other hand, it offers quick access to Pance, the Farallones mountains, prestigious universities and many event venues.
</p>

<p>
    The area is also known for its large former haciendas and mansions, some of which have been transformed into luxury hotels and wellness facilities. One of its landmark hotels is the NH Cali Royal.
</p>

<p><strong>Best for:</strong> Medical tourism • Luxury • Families • Longer stays</p>

<hr>

<h3>Local Favorites</h3>

<p>
    If you've already visited Cali once—or simply want to experience the city like a local—neighborhoods such as San Fernando, Santa Teresita, Parque del Perro, Versalles and Centenario offer an excellent balance between local life and proximity to the city's attractions.
</p>

<p>
    These neighborhoods feel less touristy while remaining conveniently located. You'll find charming boutique hotels, excellent restaurants, parks and cafés without the crowds of San Antonio or Granada.
</p>

<p>
    Versalles is home to the Sonesta Hotel, while Centenario offers excellent options such as NH Boulevard del Río, Hotel Stein Colonial and Hotel Torre de Cali. Nearby Chipichape Shopping Mall also hosts two of Cali's largest hotels: the Spiwak and Spirito.
</p>

<p>
    Tequendama deserves a special mention thanks to its proximity to the Coliseo del Pueblo, the Petronio Álvarez Festival, the World Salsa Festival, Cali Fair and Mall Plaza.
</p>

<p><strong>Best for:</strong> Returning visitors • Digital nomads • Local atmosphere</p>

<hr>

<h3>Residential Cali</h3>

<p>
    Many neighborhoods across northern, central and southern Cali offer a wonderfully authentic experience for visitors staying several weeks or even months. Areas such as La Flora, Prados del Norte, Alameda, Colseguros, El Ingenio, El Refugio and Cuarto de Legua provide excellent value and allow you to experience daily life far from the traditional tourist circuit.
</p>

<p>
    Accommodation is often more affordable, although you'll likely spend more on Uber rides as most attractions are not within walking distance.
</p>

<p><strong>Best for:</strong> Long stays • Living like a local • Budget-conscious travelers</p>

<hr>

<h3>Areas We Don't Recommend for First-Time Visitors</h3>

<p>
    Like any large city, some neighborhoods are simply better suited for visitors than others. Unless you're staying with friends or family who know the area well, we generally recommend avoiding accommodation in Downtown, Siloé and much of eastern Cali. These areas can certainly be visited with local guidance, but they are not the most practical or comfortable places to base yourself during your first trip.
</p>

