<div class="section-header" id="transportation">

```
<span class="section-kicker">
    TRANSPORTATION
</span>

<h2>
    How to Get Around Cali
</h2>
```

</div>

<p>
    Cali is a large city, and like most major cities around the world, there are several ways to get around. You can use private cars, public transportation, taxis, ride-hailing apps such as Uber, DiDi, inDrive, Yango and Cabify, or simply walk.
</p>

<p>
    The good news is that getting around Cali is usually much easier than first-time visitors expect. The important thing is understanding which option makes sense for your particular trip.
</p>

<p>
    There is no metro or passenger train in Cali. Public transportation is primarily based on buses, while ride-hailing apps and taxis are generally the most convenient options for visitors.
</p>

<h3>
    Uber and Ride-Hailing Apps
</h3>

<p>
    Uber works relatively normally in Cali, with one important detail: its legal situation in Colombia is complicated.
</p>

<p>
    The issue is not that using the Uber app as a passenger is simply "illegal." The problem is the legal status of the vehicle providing the ride. Colombian law distinguishes between private transportation and public transportation, and public passenger transportation must be provided by properly authorized operators using vehicles registered for that service. The National Transportation Statute establishes that authorized public transportation companies must operate with vehicles registered for public service.
</p>

<p>
    This is why the typical Uber vehicle in Colombia operates in a legal grey area: it is generally a privately registered vehicle being used to provide a service that resembles public passenger transportation.
</p>

<p>
    In practical terms, however, millions of rides take place through these platforms, and Uber is an extremely common way for visitors to move around Cali.
</p>

<p>
    One unusual local custom is worth knowing about.
</p>

<p>
    If you order an Uber, your driver may ask you to sit in the front passenger seat.
</p>

<p>
    This is not because Colombian drivers have suddenly decided that everyone should ride shotgun. It is partly a practical response to the legal situation surrounding private vehicles being used for app based transportation. A driver stopped by traffic authorities can potentially face serious consequences, including vehicle immobilization, when the authorities determine that a private vehicle is being used improperly for public passenger transportation.
</p>

<p>
    For that reason, many drivers prefer passengers to sit in the front.
</p>

<p>
    If your driver asks you to move to the front, don't take it personally. They're trying to reduce their own risk.
</p>

<p>
    Uber is probably the ride-hailing app most familiar to international visitors, particularly because it offers convenient electronic payment and has a strong reputation among travelers. But it is far from the only option.
</p>

<p>
    DiDi is widely used in Colombia and accepts Visa, Mastercard and American Express credit or debit cards, as well as cash in applicable cities.
</p>

<p>
    inDrive is particularly popular because passengers can propose a fare and drivers can accept it or make another offer.
</p>

<p>
    Yango and Cabify are also available options, with Cabify currently operating in Cali.
</p>

<p>
    There is no single app that is always the fastest or cheapest. Availability depends heavily on the time, location and demand.
</p>

<p>
    And this is something international visitors often underestimate: getting a car can take longer than you are used to.
</p>

<p>
    If you have a tour, appointment, restaurant reservation or flight connection, don't request your ride at the last possible minute. Give yourself some extra time for the driver to accept the trip and reach you.
</p>

<div class="editorial-quote">

```
<p>
    <strong>My local rule after years of guiding:</strong>
</p>

<h3>
    If you have somewhere important to be, don't budget only for the drive. Budget time to find the car, too.
</h3>
```

</div>

<h3>
    A Special Note for Visitors Staying in Ciudad Jardín or Pance
</h3>

<p>
    Ciudad Jardín and Pance are beautiful and comfortable areas, and they make a lot of sense for some visitors, particularly people coming to Cali for dental or medical treatments, longer stays or a closer connection to nature.
</p>

<p>
    But there is one thing many international visitors don't realize until it's too late: <strong>Ciudad Jardín and Pance are considerably farther from the traditional tourist areas than San Antonio, El Peñón or Granada.</strong>
</p>

<p>
    Cali is not an enormous city by international standards, and without heavy traffic you can cross much of it in around an hour. But "I can cross the city in an hour" is very different from "I'm five minutes from the center."
</p>

<p>
    After years of guiding tours, I've lost count of how many visitors staying in Ciudad Jardín or Pance arrived late because they assumed the Bulevar del Río was just around the corner.
</p>

<div class="editorial-quote">

```
<p>
    <strong>A local tip from Juan Camilo:</strong>
</p>

<h3>
    If you're staying in Ciudad Jardín or Pance, check the actual travel time to your activities before assuming everything is nearby.
</h3>
```

</div>

<h3>
    What Are Cars Like in Cali?
</h3>

<p>
    This deserves its own explanation, especially for visitors from the United States and Canada.
</p>

<p>
    Cars in Colombia tend to be smaller and lighter than what many North American visitors are accustomed to. Manual transmissions are still common, and you may encounter vehicles with simpler interiors, less sound insulation and fewer of the large, heavy features common in American cars.
</p>

<p>
    And there is one tiny cultural detail that deserves a warning all by itself:
</p>

<div class="editorial-quote">

```
<p>
    <strong>Americans: please don't slam the car door.</strong>
</p>

<h3>
    Seriously.
</h3>

<p>
    It isn't your fault. It's muscle memory. American cars have heavy doors that require a certain amount of force to close. Many Colombian cars have lighter doors that need much less.
</p>

<p>
    You will probably slam the door automatically at least once. Just remember this guide when you do.
</p>
```

</div>

<h3>
    Taxis
</h3>

<p>
    Taxis are everywhere in Cali and remain a perfectly normal way to get around the city.
</p>

<p>
    Depending on who you ask, taxis are either the best or worst transportation option in Cali. Their reputation comes partly from the past, when older vehicles and inconsistent service were more common.
</p>

<p>
    Today, the situation is more nuanced. You can find modern, comfortable taxis, as well as older vehicles, just as you can find both excellent and less impressive cars on ride-hailing platforms.
</p>

<p>
    The main difference for visitors is price transparency.
</p>

<p>
    Taxis use a taximeter, but the final amount can be affected by legally established surcharges, and fares can be less predictable at night, at taxi stands or when additional charges apply.
</p>

<p>
    You can hail a taxi directly from the street, and locals do it every day. However, visitors are generally more exposed to overcharging or fare misunderstandings than when using an app that displays the trip and payment information digitally.
</p>

<p>
    For most international visitors, we'd therefore recommend an app for convenience and transparency, while taxis remain a perfectly legitimate option when you need one immediately.
</p>

<h3>
    Walking Around Cali
</h3>

<p>
    Cali is surprisingly walkable if you choose the right neighborhood.
</p>

<p>
    If you're staying in San Antonio, El Peñón or Granada, many restaurants, bars, parks, historic sites and attractions are within walking distance.
</p>

<p>
    This is one of the biggest advantages of staying in the traditional tourist areas.
</p>

<p>
    If you're staying farther south, particularly in Ciudad Jardín or Pance, the experience is very different. These areas are more car oriented, distances are greater and walking between attractions is generally less practical.
</p>

<p>
    So when choosing where to stay in Cali, transportation and walkability should be part of the decision.
</p>

<h3>
    The MIO: Cali's Public Transportation System
</h3>

<p>
    The MIO is Cali's integrated public transportation system and the main public bus network in the city.
</p>

<p>
    To use the system, you'll need a rechargeable MIO card, which allows you to enter stations and board buses at designated stops throughout the city.
</p>

<p>
    The network provides extensive coverage, and you can reach a remarkable number of places using the MIO.
</p>

<p>
    The easiest way for visitors to understand the system is actually Google Maps. Enter your destination, select the public transportation option and Google Maps will show available MIO routes, stops and transfers. You don't need to understand the entire system before using it.
</p>

<p>
    The problem is time.
</p>

<p>
    A journey that takes 20 minutes in an Uber can easily take an hour or more on the MIO, depending on transfers, waiting times and traffic.
</p>

<p>
    If you want to experience Cali's public transportation system as a local, absolutely go for it. It's an interesting part of everyday city life.
</p>

<p>
    But if you're visiting for only a few days and value your time and convenience, ride-hailing will usually make much more sense.
</p>

<div class="editorial-quote">

```
<p>
    <strong>One unexpected advantage for visitors:</strong>
</p>

<h3>
    Because rides are relatively affordable for international travelers, you don't need to spend as much time navigating public transportation or waiting on the street for a bus.
</h3>

<p>
    Less time waiting around also means fewer situations where you are unnecessarily exposed to the street environment.
</p>
```

</div>

<h3>
    Getting From Cali Airport to the City
</h3>

<p>
    Cali's international airport, Alfonso Bonilla Aragón International Airport, is actually located outside Cali, near Palmira.
</p>

<p>
    There is no airport train or metro connection.
</p>

<p>
    The cheapest public option is usually one of the small buses or vans operating outside the <strong>national arrivals area</strong>. You'll find them around the national arrivals exit, not in the international arrivals area. These vehicles can take passengers toward Cali's main bus terminal and other parts of the metropolitan area for considerably less than a private ride.
</p>

<p>
    For most visitors with luggage, however, a taxi or ride-hailing service will be much more convenient.
</p>

<p>
    Uber pickups from the airport are possible, although the exact pickup procedure can depend on the current airport arrangements and your driver. Some drivers also choose to wait at the airport after dropping someone off in the hope of finding a return trip to Cali.
</p>

<p>
    Official yellow taxis are also readily available, and larger white vehicles may be available for passengers looking for additional space and comfort.
</p>

<p>
    Don't be surprised if an airport taxi costs significantly more than a normal trip across Cali. Airport transportation carries additional costs and surcharges, so it can feel expensive compared with ordinary city rides.
</p>

<h3>
    Renting a Car in Cali
</h3>

<p>
    Yes, you can rent a car in Cali.
</p>

<p>
    International rental companies operate in the city, and renting a vehicle can be considerably cheaper than in many parts of the world.
</p>

<p>
    But whether you should is another question.
</p>

<p>
    Cali's traffic can be challenging, and Colombian driving culture may feel very different from what you're accustomed to in Europe, the United States or Canada.
</p>

<p>
    Traffic is less predictable, motorcycles are common, lane discipline can feel more flexible and the general rhythm of driving takes some getting used to.
</p>

<p>
    If you've never driven in Colombia before, you may find that the freedom of having a rental car is outweighed by the stress of actually driving it.
</p>

<p>
    And there is another factor: ride-hailing is relatively inexpensive for many international visitors.
</p>

<p>
    When an Uber can take you across the city without worrying about parking, traffic, navigation or an unfamiliar driving culture, renting a car can sometimes create more problems than it solves.
</p>

<p>
    For longer stays, trips outside the city or travelers who are already comfortable driving in Latin America, a rental car can still make perfect sense.
</p>

<h3>
    So What's the Best Way to Get Around Cali?
</h3>

<p>
    For most visitors, there isn't one single answer.
</p>

<p>
    <strong>Walking</strong> is excellent when you're staying in the traditional tourist neighborhoods.
</p>

<p>
    <strong>Uber and other ride-hailing apps</strong> are usually the most convenient option for moving between neighborhoods.
</p>

<p>
    <strong>Taxis</strong> are widely available and perfectly usable, particularly when you need one immediately.
</p>

<p>
    <strong>The MIO</strong> is inexpensive and extensive, but considerably slower.
</p>

<p>
    <strong>Renting a car</strong> gives you freedom, but comes with the responsibility of dealing with Cali's traffic and driving culture.
</p>

<p>
    For a short visit, most travelers will probably end up using a combination of walking and ride-hailing apps. And honestly, that's probably the combination we'd recommend too.
</p>

