<section id="things-youll-thank-us-for-later">

```
<div class="section-header">

    <span class="section-kicker">
        LOCAL TIPS
    </span>

    <h2>
        Things You'll Thank Us For Later
    </h2>

</div>

<p>
    Cali is a welcoming city, but every destination has its own rhythms, habits and little details that aren't obvious to first time visitors.
</p>

<p>
    After years of showing international travelers around Cali, these are some of the things we wish everyone knew before arriving.
</p>

<h3>
    Keep It Simple
</h3>

<p>
    You don't need to dress like you're going on safari. Most locals keep things relatively casual, and keeping valuable jewelry, expensive watches and large amounts of cash out of sight is simply good city etiquette.
</p>

<p>
    Colombians have a great expression for this: <strong>dar papaya</strong>. Roughly translated, it means not giving someone an unnecessary opportunity.
</p>

<h3>
    Carry a Little Cash
</h3>

<p>
    Cards are widely accepted in Cali, but cash still makes everyday life easier.
</p>

<p>
    Street food, neighborhood bakeries, local markets and many small businesses may prefer Colombian pesos. Carrying around <strong>COP 50,000 to 100,000</strong> in smaller bills is usually plenty for everyday expenses.
</p>

<p>
    Smaller denominations are particularly useful. A COP 100,000 bill can be surprisingly difficult to change when you're buying a COP 5,000 snack.
</p>

<h3>
    Keep Your Passport Somewhere Safe
</h3>

<p>
    There is usually no reason to carry your original passport around the city all day.
</p>

<p>
    Keep it safely stored at your accommodation and carry a secure digital copy or photocopy of your identification and Colombian entry information instead. If you're unsure what identification you should carry for a particular situation, ask your accommodation or guide.
</p>

<h3>
    Let Locals Know You're New Here
</h3>

<p>
    One of the easiest ways to connect with people in Cali is also one of the simplest: say hello.
</p>

<p>
    <strong>Buenos días, buenas tardes, gracias and hasta luego</strong> go a surprisingly long way.
</p>

<p>
    Caleños are generally curious about visitors, and mentioning where you're from will often start a conversation. Don't be surprised if someone at a salsa club wants to know your name, where you're from or whether you can dance.
</p>

<h3>
    Learn a Little Spanish
</h3>

<p>
    English is still relatively uncommon in Cali compared with some of Colombia's more established tourist destinations.
</p>

<p>
    You don't need to become fluent. A handful of Spanish phrases and a translation app can completely change the ease of your trip.
</p>

<p>
    And locals genuinely appreciate the effort.
</p>

<h3>
    Give Cali a Weekend
</h3>

<p>
    If you can choose your dates, try to include at least one Friday or Saturday night.
</p>

<p>
    Cali's social life revolves heavily around the weekend. Salsa, live music, nightlife, restaurants, street food and neighborhood gatherings become much more active from Friday onward.
</p>

<p>
    You can visit Cali during the week and have a great time. But if you leave before the weekend, you may leave without ever seeing the city's full energy.
</p>

<div class="editorial-quote">

    <p>
        <strong>A local rule:</strong>
    </p>

    <h3>
        Cali doesn't sleep early. It wakes up after work.
    </h3>

</div>

<h3>
    Follow the Sun
</h3>

<p>
    Cali is warm year round, but the midday sun can be surprisingly intense.
</p>

<p>
    If you're planning a long walk or outdoor activity, mornings and late afternoons are usually much more comfortable. Save the hottest part of the day for lunch, museums, cafés or a slower pace.
</p>

<h3>
    Time Your Hikes
</h3>

<p>
    Cali's mountains are incredibly close to the city, but outdoor activities are better when you respect the rhythm of the environment.
</p>

<p>
    For popular hikes such as Tres Cruces, early morning is generally the best choice. Start before the sun becomes intense and you'll have a cooler, more enjoyable experience.
</p>

<p>
    For less familiar trails and mountain areas, going with someone who knows the area is always the smarter option.
</p>

<h3>
    Plan Your Way Back
</h3>

<p>
    Getting to Cali's surrounding nature is often easy. Getting back isn't always quite as simple.
</p>

<p>
    Places such as Cristo Rey, Pance and Kilómetro 18 can have fewer available ride-hailing vehicles, particularly at certain times of day.
</p>

<p>
    If you're heading somewhere outside the main urban areas, think about your return journey before you leave. Sometimes arranging transportation in advance is the difference between ending the day relaxed and standing on the side of a mountain waiting for a car.
</p>

<h3>
    Leave Some Room for Improvisation
</h3>

<p>
    This might be the most important tip of all.
</p>

<p>
    Don't schedule every hour of your trip.
</p>

<p>
    Cali rewards curiosity. A restaurant recommendation from a local, an unexpected salsa invitation, a neighborhood you hadn't planned to visit or a conversation that lasts longer than expected can easily become one of the highlights of your trip.
</p>

<div class="editorial-quote">

    <p>
        <strong>One last piece of advice:</strong>
    </p>

    <h3>
        Smile, greet people, ask questions, dance even if you're terrible at it, and give Cali enough time to surprise you.
    </h3>

    <p>
        The city's greatest attraction isn't a monument. It's the people.
    </p>

</div>
