<section id="faq">

    <div class="section-header">

        <span class="section-kicker">
            FAQ
        </span>

        <h2>
            Frequently Asked Questions About Cali
        </h2>

        <p>
            Still wondering what it is like to visit Cali, Colombia? Here are some of the questions we hear most often from international travelers.
        </p>

    </div>

    <div class="faq-list">

        <details>
            <summary>
                Is Cali, Colombia worth visiting?
            </summary>

            <p>
                Absolutely. Cali offers something that is increasingly difficult to find in Colombia's more established tourist destinations: a large, genuinely local city where salsa, Afro Colombian culture, nightlife, gastronomy and nature are part of everyday life. It may take a little more curiosity to discover than Cartagena or Medellín, but that's precisely what makes it special.
            </p>
        </details>


        <details>
            <summary>
                How many days should you spend in Cali?
            </summary>

            <p>
                Three days is a good starting point for a first visit. It gives you enough time to experience the city, explore its salsa culture, enjoy the nightlife and discover some of the surrounding nature. Four or five days are better if you want to include day trips such as San Cipriano, birdwatching, mountain experiences or the Pacific coast.
            </p>
        </details>


        <details>
            <summary>
                Is Cali expensive to visit?
            </summary>

            <p>
                Cali is generally one of Colombia's better value destinations. Accommodation, food, transportation and nightlife can be considerably more affordable than in Medellín or Cartagena, particularly when you experience the city outside the most upscale tourist venues.
            </p>
        </details>


        <details>
            <summary>
                Is Cali safe for tourists?
            </summary>

            <p>
                Cali requires the same kind of awareness you would use in any large city. Most visitors have a great experience when they stay in appropriate areas, use sensible transportation at night and avoid unnecessary displays of valuables. The city is not without risks, but it is also not the place portrayed by every alarming headline.
            </p>
        </details>


        <details>
            <summary>
                Can you drink tap water in Cali?
            </summary>

            <p>
                Cali's municipal tap water is generally considered suitable for drinking and many locals drink it every day. If you have a particularly sensitive stomach or are not accustomed to the local water, bottled or filtered water is also easy to find.
            </p>
        </details>


        <details>
            <summary>
                Do I need cash in Cali?
            </summary>

            <p>
                Carrying some Colombian pesos is a good idea. Credit cards are widely accepted in hotels, restaurants, shopping malls and many businesses, but cash remains useful for street food, neighborhood bakeries, local markets, small shops and traditional experiences. Smaller bills are especially useful.
            </p>
        </details>


        <details>
            <summary>
                Is Uber available in Cali?
            </summary>

            <p>
                Yes. Uber is widely used in Cali, particularly by international visitors. Other ride hailing apps include DiDi, InDrive, Yango and Cabify. Availability and waiting times can vary depending on the time of day and where you are staying, so allow a little extra time when you have somewhere important to be.
            </p>
        </details>


        <details>
            <summary>
                Do people speak English in Cali?
            </summary>

            <p>
                English is not widely spoken in Cali compared with some of Colombia's more tourist oriented destinations. Hotels, restaurants and tour companies may have English speaking staff, but you should not assume that everyone will speak English. A few Spanish phrases and a translation app will make your visit considerably easier.
            </p>
        </details>


        <details>
            <summary>
                What is the best time of year to visit Cali?
            </summary>

            <p>
                Cali is a year round destination because temperatures remain relatively stable throughout the year. The drier periods are generally more convenient for outdoor activities, while the wetter months can offer excellent conditions for nature experiences. August is especially interesting for the Petronio Álvarez Festival, October for salsa events, and late December for Feria de Cali.
            </p>
        </details>


        <details>
            <summary>
                What is Cali famous for?
            </summary>

            <p>
                Cali is best known internationally as the Salsa Capital of the World, but salsa is only part of the story. The city is also famous for its Afro Colombian culture, vibrant nightlife, distinctive local food, surrounding mountains and biodiversity, birdwatching, rivers and its unusually strong connection between urban life and nature.
            </p>
        </details>


        <details>
            <summary>
                Is Cali good for solo travelers?
            </summary>

            <p>
                Yes, particularly if you stay in a social and walkable area such as San Antonio, El Peñón or Granada. Hostels can be especially useful for solo travelers because they provide an easy way to meet other visitors and often organize salsa classes and social activities. As anywhere else, use sensible precautions when exploring at night.
            </p>
        </details>


        <details>
            <summary>
                Is Cali good for a first trip to Colombia?
            </summary>

            <p>
                It depends on what you want from Colombia. If you're looking for beaches, Cartagena may make more sense. If you want a major international city and a polished tourism infrastructure, Medellín may be easier. But if you want salsa, nightlife, Afro Colombian culture, incredible food and close access to mountains, rivers and biodiversity, Cali is one of the most rewarding places to start.
            </p>
        </details>

    </div>

